  Recommendation: [1momit it from CSV, include it in JSON[0m as [38;5;153mtotal_cents[39m alongside [38;5;153mrows[39m. CSV's value is being a clean table
  you can load into anything; JSON has an envelope to put a total in without polluting the records. If you'd rather the
  two formats agree with each other, the separate-field option is the consistent one.

  Also assumed unless you say otherwise: both formats carry [1minteger cents[0m, not formatted dollars — the [38;5;153m/100[39m in
  [38;5;153mrender_text[39m is a display concern, and exporting [38;5;153m"10.50"[39m invites float rounding downstream.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the daily total appear in the exported CSV and JSON? → Omit in CSV, field in JSON (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — CSV stays a clean table, JSON carries the envelope.

  Last thing that's a real fork; the rest I'll just propose. [1mHow the command picks the format.[0m

  - [1mExplicit [38;5;153m--format csv|json[39m, required [38;5;153m--out PATH[0m — the cron line says exactly what it produces. Nothing to infer,
    nothing to get wrong when someone writes to [38;5;153mreport.txt[39m or a path with no extension.
  - [1mInfer from the [38;5;153m--out[39m extension[0m — shorter to type, and [38;5;153m--format[39m can override. The failure mode is quiet: an
    unrecognized or missing extension either errors anyway or silently picks a default, so you end up specifying the
    flag in scripts regardless.
  - [1mExplicit [38;5;153m--format[39m, [38;5;153m--out[39m optional, defaults to stdout[0m — lets you pipe CSV/JSON as well as write it. But writing a
    file is the stated point of this command, and [38;5;153msummarize[39m already covers stdout.

  Recommendation: the [1mfirst[0m — both flags explicit and required. This is invoked from a cron line, where being
  unambiguous beats being terse, and extension inference can be added later as pure sugar without breaking anyone.
